response_text:
{
  "bioavailability_prediction": "low",
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "revision_action": "strengthen",
  "supportive_card_ids": ["C13","C21","C27","C67","C08","C34","C10","C32","C57","C82","C85","C106","C119","C93","C74"],
  "contradictory_card_ids": ["C17","C20","C47"],
  "prediction_basis_card_ids": ["C13","C21","C27","C67","C08","C34","C10","C82","C85","C119"],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Andrographolide, the closest diterpenoid comparator, had poor oral bioavailability in humans and low absolute values in rodents (9.27% in mice, 0.3% at 300 mg/kg, 1.19% for an analogue), supporting F below 20% for the query's complex diterpenoid acid.",
      "card_ids": ["C13","C21","C27","C67"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Docetaxel, another complex diterpenoid, has low absolute oral bioavailability in humans and 0.315% in rats, supporting low F for the query.",
      "card_ids": ["C08","C34"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Withaferin A had poor oral bioavailability in humans (L1 gold), although L2 rodent values vary; the human outcome supports low.",
      "card_ids": ["C10","C32","C57"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 exposure records show extremely low oral plasma exposures for terpenoid acids: pachymic acid Cmax 36-91 pg/mL and AUC 277-1210 pg·h/mL after oral dosing, consistent with low oral bioavailability for terpenoid acids.",
      "card_ids": ["C82","C85","C106","C119","C107"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Abiraterone acetate and ginsenoside CK also show very low oral exposures (pg/mL Cmax and 147.5 ng·h/mL AUClast), reinforcing low oral exposure for complex terpenoid and steroid scaffolds.",
      "card_ids": ["C93","C94","C74"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "oral_auc_cmax_exposure",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C82","C85","C106","C119","C107","C101","C93","C94","C74"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental oral bioavailability measurement for the query molecule.",
    "L3 exposure records are for other molecules, not the query; no matched oral-versus-IV comparison for the query.",
    "No formulation, dose, or food-effect information is available for the query.",
    "The query's carboxylic acid, acetyl ester, and prenyl side chain may alter absorption and first-pass metabolism, but this is not directly evidenced.",
    "Withaferin A L2 data show species- and dose-dependent oral bioavailability, so the strength of that analogy is uncertain.",
    "L4 and L5 permeability, efflux, and intestinal metabolism evidence is not yet available."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "The query is a complex tetracyclic diterpenoid acid with MW 516, a fully ionized carboxylic acid at pH 7.4, an acetyl ester, two hydroxyls, and a prenyl side chain. The closest transferable L1 gold outcomes are low or poor in humans: andrographolide, docetaxel, and withaferin A. L2 context reinforces low absolute values in rodents for these comparators. L3 exposure records show extremely low oral plasma exposures for terpenoid acids, notably pachymic acid with Cmax 36-91 pg/mL and AUC 277-1210 pg·h/mL after oral dosing, and abiraterone acetate with Cmax about 55 pg/mL. Conflicting high withaferin A rodent values (87% and 32.4%) reduce confidence but do not overturn the human gold outcomes or provide direct query evidence. No affirmative evidence supports F at or above 20%. The prediction remains low."
}

